## Configure NTP

HPC systems rely on synchronized clocks throughout the system and the NTP
protocol can be used to facilitate this synchronization. To enable NTP services
on the head node with a specific server `${ntp_server}`, and allow this server
to serve as a local time server for the cluster, issue the following:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Enable NTP services on head node -->
```bash
{{ pkg_install }} chrony
systemctl enable chronyd.service
echo "local stratum 10" >> /etc/chrony.conf
echo "server ${ntp_server}" >> /etc/chrony.conf
echo "allow all" >> /etc/chrony.conf
systemctl restart chronyd.service
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

::: {.tip}
**Note:** Note that the "allow all" option specified for the chrony time daemon
allows all servers on the local network to be able to synchronize with the head
node. Alternatively, you can restrict access to fixed IP ranges and an example
config line allowing access to a local class B subnet is as follows:

```bash
allow 192.168.0.0/16
```
:::
